# SPDX-License-Identifier: AGPL-3.0-only
#
# WHERE 4.5:1 IS THE ANSWER AND 7:1 IS NOT ASKED FOR (#447).
#
# scripts/a11y-check.cjs runs axe twice per surface per theme. The first run is
# the GATE: WCAG 2 A + AA, and a violation is a red build with no exceptions and
# no file like this one. The second run is `color-contrast-enhanced` — AAA, 7:1 —
# which this project MEASURES rather than gates, because AAA on body text and AAA
# on a 12px timestamp beside it are different decisions and only one of them is
# worth taking.
#
# So: every AAA failure is reported, and a failure whose element is not named here
# FAILS THE RUN. That is the opposite way round from an ordinary lint suppression
# file, and deliberately — a new element that misses 7:1 has to be looked at and
# either fixed or written down with a reason, rather than joining a silent
# majority. `--disable-rules color-contrast-enhanced` would have been one line and
# would have measured nothing.
#
# FORMAT: `<selector> <min-ratio> <reason>`. The selector must contain no spaces
# (a comma list is fine); the element is tested with `Element.matches`.
#
# ⚠ THE NUMBER IS WHAT STOPS THIS BEING A MUTE. An allowlist of bare selectors
# accepts 6.9:1 and 2:1 equally, so a token that regressed INSIDE the allowed band
# would pass forever. Each floor here is the WORST ratio that element was actually
# measured at, across both themes and every background it was seen on, rounded
# down to one decimal — so a regression of more than a rounding error is red even
# though AA would still pass it. The lane prints `BELOW-FLOOR` with both numbers
# when that happens.
#
# ⚠ AND THE FLOOR IS NOT THE WCAG FLOOR. AA (4.5:1) is enforced twice over and
# neither enforcement is here: scripts/check-tokens.mjs asserts every declared
# token pair at 4.5:1 in BOTH themes on every `npm run build`, and the axe AA run
# above this one re-measures the RENDERED result. An entry here means "held to AA,
# on purpose, and currently at N". It never means "unmeasured".
#
# HOW THIS FILE WAS WRITTEN, and how to rewrite it:
#
#     A11Y_CONTRAST_REPORT=1 sh scripts/a11y-e2e.sh
#
# which measures instead of gating and prints one `aaacand` line per element class
# and colour pair with the worst ratio seen. Every number below came from that run
# (axe-core 4.13.0, Chromium 124, 11 surfaces × 3 themes): 46 element classes, all
# of them between 4.66:1 and 6.97:1 — that is, every AAA shortfall in this client
# is a LABEL at comfortably better than AA, and not one thing is close to failing.
# That is the finding this file records.
#
# THREE MORE ENTRIES (`.field-hint`, `.setup-step-n`, `.setup-step-name`) were
# added later, when the day-one wizard (`#/setup`, mailroom#447 follow-up) joined
# the surface list as its own DENIED failures rather than a fresh report run — the
# ratio (5.94:1, floored to 5.9) is the same `--fg-muted` tone already documented
# above at 5.9 on half a dozen other elements, not a new finding.
#
# THE THREE FAMILIES BELOW ARE THREE TOKENS, and the entries are the elements each
# one is drawn on. If a fourth family appears, the honest move is to ask whether
# it is a label at all before adding lines to it.

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. THE MUTED LABEL TONE (--fg-muted). Counts, timestamps, aliases, previews,
#    state lines, the header sentence: the second thing on a row, never the
#    thing itself. tokens.css declares this tone against --bg, --bg-selected,
#    --bg-read and --bg-unread at 4.5:1 and check-tokens.mjs holds it there.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.nav-badge 4.6 rail unread count; also the one drawn on the selected entry (--bg-selected)
.nav-badge-split 5.3 the split half of a rail badge
.nav-heading 5.3 rail section heading, including its disclosure button form
.nav-item-muted 5.3 a rail entry for something not syncing
.sidebar-footer 5.3 the rail's footer line
.desk-heading-hint 5.3 the hint beside an area heading
.desk-line-side 5.3 the two counts either side of the line
.desk-line-value 5.3 the line's printed threshold (its aria-valuetext is the real answer)
.desk-drawer-sweep 5.3 a drawer's auto-archive sentence
.list-count 5.3 the message list's count
.setting-scope-word 5.3 a settings row's scope word
.setting-fact 5.3 one clause of a settings row's state line
.menu-group>legend 5.3 a radio group's legend
.viewer-fact 5.3 a fact on the attachment viewer's top bar
.viewer-thumb-kind 4.6 the file-type caption on a viewer thumbnail, incl. the current one
# ⚠ THE NEXT SEVEN WERE 5.5 UNTIL mailroom#514, AND THE NUMBER CHANGED BECAUSE
# THE MEASUREMENT DID, NOT BECAUSE THE COLOURS DID. Adding two desk surfaces to
# the lane (the multi-line row layout, and the largest text size) put the
# pointer, after a click in Settings, over a desk row on the way back — so for
# the first time these three elements were measured on `--bg-hover` (#eceff1)
# rather than on `--bg-read`/`--bg-unread`. `--fg-muted` on `--bg-hover` is
# 5.15:1; on `--bg-read` it is 5.59:1, which is where 5.5 came from.
#
# This is #447's lesson repeating exactly: "a pair that exists only because two
# rules meet in the cascade is invisible to the token checker and plain to a
# browser." The hovered row is a state every one of these elements is drawn in
# every day; nobody had ever measured it. So the floor moves to the real worst
# case, tokens.css gains an `@contrast fg-muted/bg-hover` line (which holds the
# pair at AA, 4.5:1, from now on, and it clears that at 5.15), and the four
# message-list entries move with them because the same two rules meet there.
#
# NOT "lowered to make the lane green": 5.15 is the measurement, the pair is
# still comfortably AA, and the floor is still a floor — a token change that
# took it to 5.0 would go red tomorrow.
.desk-preview 5.1 the desk row's preview text, including on a hovered row (--bg-hover)
.desk-alias 5.1 the desk row's "arrived at" alias, including on a hovered row
.desk-time 5.1 the desk row's time, including on a hovered row
.sender 5.1 a READ message-list row's sender (unread is --fg), including on a hovered row
.subject 5.1 a READ message-list row's subject, including on a hovered row
.preview 5.1 the message-list row's preview text, including on a hovered row
.row-meta>time 5.1 the message-list row's time, including on a hovered row
.desk-header 5.9 the desk's one live region, the header sentence
.desk-tab-count 5.9 the count printed inside a desk tab
.desk-tab 5.9 a desk tab's label, selected or not
.field-label 5.9 a form field's visible label
.field-hint 5.9 explanatory text under a control (first seen on the day-one wizard's step 1, when it has only an inbox to report on)
.setup-step-n 5.9 a not-yet-current step's number in the day-one wizard's step list (--fg-muted; the current step is --fg, see .setup-step.is-current)
.setup-step-name 5.9 a not-yet-current step's name in the same list
.compose-from-reason 5.9 why the composer chose this From address
.compose-attach-label 5.9 the composer's attachment label
.compose-sendline>span 5.9 the composer's send line note
.compose-save-state 5.9 the composer's autosave state
.message-address 5.9 an address under a name in the reading pane
.message-meta 5.9 a reading-pane meta line
.pane-label-add 5.9 the reading pane's add-a-label control
.attachment-size 5.9 an attachment's size beside its name
# The withheld chip's reason line, first linted when #675's held-inline surface
# drew a chip at all (no earlier surface had a quarantined part): the same tone
# as .attachment-size, measured 5.94:1.
.attachment-withheld-reason 5.9 why a held file is held ("the scanner flagged this" / "could not be checked")
# The composer's attachment tiles, first measured when #612's reload surface
# opened a FORWARD (the only composer with tiles) — the same tone as
# .attachment-size, on the tile's --bg-sunken, hence 5.34:1 floored to 5.3.
.compose-tile-size 5.3 a composer attachment tile's size
.compose-tile-origin 5.3 a composer attachment tile's "from the message" note
.sender-popover-address 5.9 the sender popover's address line
.sender-popover-alias 5.9 the sender popover's "writes to" line
.sender-popover-section>h3 5.9 a sender-popover section heading

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. THE ACCENT AS TEXT (--accent on a background, --accent-fg on --accent).
#    Words that are also controls. tokens.css declares both pairs at 4.5:1.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.link-button 5.1 a words-only button (Retry, Details, Undo, Change, and the two compose toggles)
.desk-line-move 6.6 the line's "stricter"/"looser"
.compose-button 6.6 the rail's Compose button (--accent-fg on --accent)
.button-primary 6.6 a primary button (--accent-fg on --accent)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. THE WARNING TONE (--danger on --bg-raised). It is never the only carrier:
#    the sentence itself says what is wrong, and the row's accessible name
#    repeats it (ui/desk/rowName.ts warningText).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
.sender-warning 6.6 the sender-warning panel in the reading pane
# The withheld chip's Release button (#675's surface, as above): --danger on
# --bg-raised, 8.0:1 light and 6.69:1 dark (computed from tokens.css; the first
# run measured 6.96:1 on the browser's button face, before the button was given
# a background of its own). Never the only carrier: the chip's accessible name
# says "withheld" and the button says what it does.
.attachment-withheld>.button-quiet 6.6 the Release button on a withheld chip
# The rest of the chip is the same tone on the same face (dark: #f28b82 on
# #1e2228, measured 6.68:1): the held file's name and its WITHHELD pill.
.attachment-withheld-open>.attachment-name 6.6 a held file's name on its withheld chip
.attachment-withheld-state 6.6 the WITHHELD pill on a withheld chip
